Ghostlane's one change to sing-box v1.13.14 (patch -p1 in the module's tree;
scripts/patched-singbox.sh applies it for every build: the CLI, Android, the
desktops and the iOS Cores).

A Reality line opens on current Xray servers.

1. The hello keeps its X25519MLKEM768 key share. sing-box strips it from every
   Reality hello (since its commit dbdcce20a8, 2025, no option, no reason
   given; upstream `testing` still does on 2026-11-11). XTLS/REALITY 8cdf7bf
   (2026-09-08, in Xray-core from v26.9.8) forwards a hello without one ahead
   of X25519 to the camouflage target, so the line never opens. Xray-core's
   own client never strips it.
2. A fingerprint whose hello has no such share in this uTLS (firefox, safari,
   ios, edge, 360, qq, android; `random` four times in five) is sent as
   Chrome's, the only hello here that has it.
3. The hello announces 26.7.11 instead of 1.8.1. Xray-core v26.7.x servers
   with no minClientVer configured refuse anything below 26.3.27.

Measured on 2026-11-11 against the official Xray-linux-64 builds, VLESS +
Reality + Vision: unpatched, the chrome hello opens on v26.4.25 only and is
refused by v26.7.11 and v26.11.9; patched, chrome, firefox, safari, ios,
random and randomized open on all three.

4. A hello nobody answers is retried once with the short hello. The hello of
   1 to 3 is about 1.8 KB, two TCP segments, and some networks deliver the
   first segment and never the second (ghostlane-project/ghostlane#166): the
   server waits for the end of the hello, and every dial hangs until its
   timeout. The TLS dialer (common/tls/client.go, which already dials again
   for ECH) watches a Reality hello for 4 s from the start of the handshake.
   With not one byte of answer it closes that connection and opens one more
   with the hello sing-box sends without this patch: the fingerprint's own
   with the X25519MLKEM768 group and share taken out, which fits one
   segment. Any byte ends the watch: a server that answered is never
   retried.
   The outcome is kept per server (address, port, server name) for 10
   minutes, for at most 64 servers, process-wide. Where the short hello
   opened the line, later dials send it first. Where it went unanswered too
   or met a real certificate (a server from Xray-core v26.9.8 on hands it to
   its camouflage target), later dials send the long hello alone, unwatched,
   as before. A retry that ends in a reset, a close or a failed connect is
   not kept, and a failure is not kept over a short hello that worked.
   NetworkManager.ResetNetwork (route/network.go) empties the memory. Two
   debug lines: the retry, and the outcome kept. No option.
   What the retry gives up: its connection is keyed by X25519 alone, as every
   Reality connection of an unpatched sing-box is; REALITY's authentication
   is the same for both hellos. Not covered: a handshake on a connection the
   TLS dialer did not make (the gRPC transport of a `with_grpc` build).

